\textbf{Empirical Findings from Completed Trials:}
A total of $27$ valid behavioral trials have been completed across two temporal blocks. Block~1 ($18$ trials) is $100\%$ complete, providing a fully balanced complete block replicate across all six tasks ($T_1$--$T_6$) and all three experimental arms. In Block~2, $9$ valid trials have been completed.
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Empirical Ceiling Effect on Task Success:} Across all $27$ completed valid trials, the autonomous agent (\texttt{gemini-3.1-flash-lite} with \texttt{use\_vision=False}) achieved $100\%$ task success ($27/27$) under independent ground-truth evaluation. Because there were zero observed task failures across all conditions, binary task completion displays zero variance across arms ($\chi^2 = 0.000, p = 1.000$). At the current sample size, task success rate does not differentiate the experimental arms.
    \item \textbf{Trajectory Length and Step Efficiency:} In the unaugmented baseline (\textsc{ABS}), the agent completed tasks in an average of $6.50$ steps (median: 5.0). Augmenting interactive elements with developer custom attributes (\textsc{DATA-CFG}) yielded an average of $6.00$ steps (median: 4.0), while additive accessibility labels (\textsc{ARIA}) required an average of $7.00$ steps (median: 5.0). A one-way Analysis of Variance (ANOVA) across the three arms indicates that step count differences are not statistically significant ($F(2, 24) = 0.139, p = 0.871$, critical $F_{0.05} = 3.403$).
    \item \textbf{Deconfounded Duration Comparison in Block 1:} When isolating Block~1 (the $100\%$ balanced replicate with $n=6$ per arm across all tasks), mean durations are closely aligned: \textsc{ABS} ($32.07$s), \textsc{ARIA} ($33.07$s), and \textsc{DATA-CFG} ($44.58$s), with \textsc{DATA-CFG} experiencing higher latency due to longer prompt serialization buffers.
    \item \textbf{Grounded Refusal Fidelity (Task T6):} Across completed trials of the adversarial stock probe (Task T6), the agent consistently inspected product availability and emitted valid grounded refusals without placing unauthorized orders. On Run 26 Attempt 4, the agent completed Task T6 in 10 steps, producing an explicit grounded refusal (\textit{``manufacturer warranty duration is not explicitly stated anywhere on the website''}) with zero duration hallucinations.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Infrastructure Availability and Error Segregation}
During the execution of Block~2, Run~26 (\textsc{ABS}, Task~T6) encountered repeated upstream capacity spikes on the Google AI Studio public endpoint, returning HTTP \texttt{503 UNAVAILABLE}:
\begin{quote}
\texttt{503 UNAVAILABLE: This model is currently experiencing high demand. Spikes in demand are usually temporary. Please try again later.}
\end{quote}
In strict adherence to the pre-registered protocol:
\begin{itemize}
    \item All three initial attempts on Run~26 were classified as \texttt{status="infrastructure\_aborted"} with score $0.0$, strictly quarantined from behavioral denominators.
    \item Full telemetry from Attempt~1 (step 13 abort) was saved to \texttt{results/3arm\_runs/run\_026.json}.
    \item Attempt~2 (step 2 abort upon initial resumption) was saved to \texttt{results/3arm\_runs/run\_026\_attempt\_2.json}.
    \item Attempt~3 (step 14 abort after extensive DOM exploration) was saved to \texttt{results/3arm\_runs/run\_026\_attempt\_3.json}.
    \item Attempt~4 executed after service stabilization and completed cleanly at step 10 (\texttt{run\_026\_attempt\_4.json}), achieving verified task success ($1.0$).
\end{itemize}

\section{Discussion, Scope, and Threats to Validity}
\label{sec:discussion}

\subsection{Discussion of Observations}
The architectural findings regarding the DOM serialization gap highlight a fundamental misalignment between modern web standards and autonomous agent observation pipelines. While web developers invest heavily in Schema.org JSON-LD structured data for search engine discovery, popular agent frameworks discard these data structures during tree simplification. Similarly, developer custom attributes (\texttt{data-*}) that encode rich application state in Single Page Applications are stripped by default attribute whitelisting.

These findings suggest that designing the ``Agentic Web'' does not require inventing novel machine formats (e.g., \texttt{llms.txt}); rather, it requires standardizing the client-side serialization pipeline so that agents can ingest existing structured markup already embedded in modern web documents.

\subsection{Scope and Threats to Validity}
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Scope and Bounded Generalization:} The behavioral findings are situated within a single-model evaluation (\texttt{gemini-3.1-flash-lite}) operating on a self-hosted e-commerce application. The observed ceiling effect and step invariance reflect model performance under clear visible typography; they should not be extrapolated to arbitrarily complex enterprise portals, heavily obfuscated DOMs, or alternative foundation models without multi-model replication.
    \item \textbf{Internal Validity:} Upstream API availability spikes (HTTP 503) introduce temporal clustering risks. We mitigate this through randomized block scheduling and fail-closed halting guards.
    \item \textbf{Construct Validity:} If the serializer prunes attributes inconsistently across HTML element types, the observed treatment effect could reflect serializer heuristics rather than model reasoning capabilities. We verified treatment delivery on actual prompt strings (\texttt{test\_agent\_prompt\_pipeline.py}) to confirm attribute presence.
    \item \textbf{Statistical Conclusion Validity:} Due to sample size limitations ($N=24$/arm planned; $N=27$ valid completed), non-significant contrasts cannot establish equivalence. The study is powered strictly as an exploratory pilot investigation.
\end{itemize}

\section{Reproducibility, Ethics, and Open Science}
\label{sec:reproducibility}

\subsection{Computational Reproducibility}
The entire experimental infrastructure is packaged for self-contained, offline reproducibility:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Deterministic Randomization:} Schedule generation and trial order are controlled via fixed pseudorandom seeds (Seed: 42).
    \item \textbf{Zero Financial Expenditure Policy:} The research pipeline enforces a hard spending cap of USD \$0.00, operating within public free-tier quotas and employing local client-side budget guards (\texttt{LocalBudgetGuard}). While local counters report \$0.00 billed spend, provider-side billing status is reported as locally bounded rather than externally audited.
    \item \textbf{Local Web Fixtures:} The target e-commerce store runs on a lightweight, standalone Python HTTP server, eliminating external network dependencies, third-party tracking, or live store price volatility.
    \item \textbf{Offline Test Battery:} The complete software suite includes automated unit and regression tests executing in $<0.4$ seconds without network access.
\end{itemize}

\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

This paper formalizes the DOM serialization gap in autonomous browser agents, establishing that default observation pipelines discard rich structured metadata (Schema.org JSON-LD and developer custom attributes) prior to model prompt construction. We have developed and validated an experimentally controlled, deconfounded 3-arm evaluation protocol ($N=72$) designed to test whether additive WAI-ARIA and configured \texttt{data-*} markup causally improve agent decision-making under identical visible text. Preliminary results from 27 completed trials demonstrate that when rendered visible text is already complete and clear, additive semantic markup yields no statistically significant improvement in task success or trajectory efficiency for frontier flash models. By providing hardened ground-truth verifiers, immutable telemetry preservation, and literature-validated statistical pipelines, this work establishes a rigorous empirical foundation for evaluating the next generation of autonomous web agents.

